{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_4","type":"agent_message","text":"{\"submission\":\"update.json\",\"summary\":\"One more check with a real safety stake fitted in before the budget closed: liveBootstrap writes the current term as one unconditionally, and if that could be reached on a node that had already adopted a higher term it would be a second route to the monotonicity violation this run witnessed through the heartbeat path. It cannot, and the reason is a protection rather than an absence of one. The write goes through BootstrapCluster, which asks first whether any state exists and refuses when the persisted current term is above zero, and since every term adoption in this package persists before it stores, there is no way to have observed any term without that guard firing, whether through a vote request, an append, or the node's own election. I recorded the negative with its guard and with the precise shape it shares with the finding I did record: the same invariant, no check at the point of the write, and a different protection upstream - which is exactly the kind of protection a later change could remove without noticing, and naming it here is what makes that visible. The limits are stated: the check is source-level, it covers the two bootstrap entry points rather than every term writer, and it does not alter the witnessed regression, which comes from concurrent writers rather than from an unguarded single write. That closes the useful work I can do inside this budget, and the record stands as it was otherwise: the demonstrated behaviours with their receipts and controls, the protocol-gate bypass on the heartbeat fast path, the verification count drawing on sources outside the voter set and scoped to its single producer, the conditional account of the regression under application order at the shared store key, and the several scoped negatives - the log cache under in-tree producers, the commit index arithmetic, and now the bootstrap term write - each with the boundary that makes it honest.\"}"}}
